 0 R/Count 9/First 889 0 R/Last 890 0 R/Next 874 0 R/Parent 866 0 R/Prev 868 0 R/Title(II. Colorado II was correct and well-reasoned.)>>endobj888 0 obj<</D[708 0 R/XYZ 155 490 0.0]/S/GoTo>>endobj889 0 obj<</A 891 0 R/Count 2/First 892 0 R/Last 893 0 R/Next 894 0 R/Parent 872 0 R/Title(A. Congress may regulate campaign contributions.)>>endobj890 0 obj<</A 895 0 R/Parent 872 0 R/Prev 896 0 R/Title(D. This Court correctly held that coordinated party expenditure limits are properly tailored.)>>endobj895 0 obj<</D[581 0 R/XYZ 155 490 0.0]/S/GoTo>>endobj896 0 obj<</A 897 0 R/Count 3/First 898 0 R/Last 899 0 R/Next 890 0 R/Parent 872 0 R/Prev 894 0 R/Title(C. The party coordinated expenditure limits are not fundamentally different.)>>endobj897 0 obj<</D[716 0 R/XYZ 155 562 0.0]/S/GoTo>>endobj898 0 obj<</A 900 0 R/Next 901 0 R/Parent 896 0 R/Title(1. Coordinated party expenditure limits are a benefit that enhances, not impedes, political parties core functions.)>>endobj899 0 obj<</A 902 0 R/Parent 896 0 R/Prev 901 0 R/Title(3. Petitioners do not articulate a constitutional basis to cabin unlimited coordinated expenditures to party committees.)>>endobj894 0 obj<</A 903 0 R/Next 896 0 R/Parent 872 0 R/Prev 889 0 R/Title(B. Coordinated expenditures are functionally identical to contributions and may be regulated as such.)>>endobj903 0 obj<</D[714 0 R/XYZ 155 597 0.0]/S/GoTo>>endobj902 0 obj<</D[579 0 R/XYZ 155 418 0.0]/S/GoTo>>endobj901 0 obj<</A 904 0 R/Next 899 0 R/Parent 896 0 R/Prev 898 0 R/Title(2. Eliminating coordinated expenditure limits would weaken parties.)>>endobj904 0 obj<</D[721 0 R/XYZ 155 268 0.0]/S/GoTo>>endobj900 0 obj<</D[717 0 R/XYZ 155 441 0.0]/S/GoTo>>endobj891 0 obj<</D[708 0 R/XYZ 155 285 0.0]/S/GoTo>>endobj892 0 obj<</A 905 0 R/Next 893 0 R/Parent 889 0 R/Title(1. Contribution limits impose only moderate burdens on speech.)>>endobj893 0 obj<</A 906 0 R/Parent 889 0 R/Prev 892 0 R/Title(2. Contribution